cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software in academic work, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "QEL: Quantum Entanglement Link"
abstract: >-
  A quantum-network simulation stack covering density-matrix states, noise, error
  correction, QKD and repeater placement over a mesh topology. Areas of substantive
  result include: an exact minimum-weight T-join surface-code decoder that removes the
  PyMatching dependency on the threshold path and reproduces a threshold near p = 0.007;
  a proof that -log W rather than -log F is the correct additive routing weight for
  Werner-state entanglement swapping; a chance-constrained repeater-placement
  formulation over continuous coherence-time uncertainty; and a decoy-state key-rate
  implementation calibrated against a published dataset.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Negenborn"
    given-names: "Lukas"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-5887-4257"
repository-code: "https://github.com/KELLERBABG/QEL"
url: "https://kellersystems.dev"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - quantum networks
  - quantum key distribution
  - decoy state
  - surface code
  - error correction
  - repeater placement
  - entanglement swapping
  - simulation
notes: >-
  Scope, stated in the software's own documentation and repeated here because it
  governs what the numbers mean: these are simulator results. Nothing in this
  repository has been validated against physical hardware, and the measured values do
  not predict what hardware would achieve. The published-dataset calibration
  reproduces two of four transcribed statements from its source, with the two
  differences reported rather than tuned away.
